# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
# AUTO-GENERATED Phase-4.
GLOSSARY_ADD = [
    {"term": "DLI", "defn": "DLI (daily light integral) is the total quantity of light that a plant receives and can use in one day, in mol/m²/day. You calculate DLI from the light intensity (PPFD) and the hours of light. Thus two different intensities can give the same DLI with different photoperiods.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Photoperiod", "defn": "The photoperiod is the number of hours of light and the number of hours of darkness in each day. You write it as hours of light/hours of darkness. For example, 18/6 is for vegetative growth, and 12/12 causes flowering to start and to continue.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "The flip", "defn": "The flip is the change of the photoperiod from 18/6 to 12/12, to cause flowering. The lights are on for a smaller number of hours. Thus the DLI decreases by approximately 28% if the PPFD does not change.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Acclimation", "defn": "Acclimation is the change in which a plant adapts to a higher light intensity during some weeks. The plant makes more chloroplasts, thicker leaf cuticles and a higher density of enzymes. Thus it can use the higher light intensity safely.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Photoinhibition", "defn": "Photoinhibition is the condition in which a leaf absorbs more light energy than it can use. As a result, photosynthesis decreases and damage to the leaf occurs.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Bleaching", "defn": "Bleaching is a change of color in which the tissue becomes white or almost white. It occurs usually at the tips in the top of the canopy. Stress from light and oxidation causes it, and it is the result of photoinhibition that you can see.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Reactive oxygen species", "defn": "Reactive oxygen species are molecules that cause damage to the leaf tissue and cause bleaching. The plant makes these molecules when it absorbs light energy that it cannot use. This occurs when there is too much light, or when there is not sufficient CO2 to use the energy.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Calvin cycle", "defn": "The Calvin cycle is the half of photosynthesis that uses CO2. It changes the light energy that the plant absorbs into sugar. If the concentration of CO2 is too low, this stage cannot operate at the same rate as the light reactions.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "VPD", "defn": "VPD (vapor pressure deficit) is a value in kPa that you calculate from the temperature and the humidity together. It controls the rate at which a plant transpires. The procedure here uses a VPD of 1.2 to 1.5 kPa.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "CO2 supplementation", "defn": "CO2 supplementation is a procedure in which you add carbon dioxide to the air. The concentration of carbon dioxide is then more than the usual value in the air (approximately 400 to 600 ppm). It agrees with a high light intensity, and it is necessary to prevent bleaching at intensities such as 1200 or 1500 µmol/m²/s.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Chloroplast", "defn": "A chloroplast is the structure in a cell that does photosynthesis. During acclimation, plants make more chloroplasts. Thus they can use a higher light intensity.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Integrated Pest Management (IPM)", "defn": "Integrated Pest Management (IPM) is a loop of tasks that you do again and again. You examine the pests, you make a decision, and you control the pests. You use the weakest method that is sufficient, and you use a stronger method only when it is necessary.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Scouting", "defn": "Scouting is a regular visual inspection of the plants and the traps. You do the inspection to find pests, damage or disease when they first occur.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Action threshold", "defn": "An action threshold is a specified quantity of pests that starts a specified control procedure. For example, the quantity can be one pest that you see, or pests in more than 3% of a room.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Biological control (biocontrol)", "defn": "Biological control (biocontrol) is the procedure in which you release predators, which are insects or mites that eat pests. The predators do not cause damage to the plant. When no prey is available, the number of predators decreases.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Generalist and specialist predator", "defn": "A generalist predator eats many types of pest and also eats pollen. It stays in the crop when there is no prey. A specialist predator eats one type of pest quickly, but its number decreases when there is no prey of that type.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Foliar spray", "defn": "A foliar spray is a liquid treatment that you apply to the leaves and the stems, and not to the root zone.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Drench", "defn": "A drench is a treatment that you apply to the substrate and the root zone, and not to the foliage.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "REI (re-entry interval)", "defn": "The REI (re-entry interval) is the mandatory time that you must wait after a spray before a person can safely go into the room again. A label on the door shows the REI.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Withholding period", "defn": "The withholding period is the time that is necessary after a treatment before you can harvest the crop as a product. Thus the residues stay less than the limits.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Quarantine", "defn": "Quarantine is the procedure in which you keep new plants apart from the primary crop. When you receive the plants, clean them. Hold them for a minimum of 2 weeks and monitor them before you put them with the primary crop.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Frass", "defn": "Frass is waste from insects and small pieces of material. During scouting, you can see frass as one of the first signs of pests.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Sticky trap", "defn": "A sticky trap is a sheet with a tacky surface, usually yellow or a different color. It catches pests in the air. Thus it gives an indication of pests in the first stage, and you can monitor the number of pests.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Sachet / release box", "defn": "A sachet or release box is a container, such as a pouch, that you hang on the plant. It releases predator insects onto the plant. It keeps the carrier material out of the irrigation system.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Exclusion", "defn": "Exclusion is the procedure in which you control the persons and the items that go into a room with pests. It stops pests that move from one room to a different room.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Tank mix", "defn": "A tank mix is a mixture of two or more products in one spray tank. Mix only the products that the label lets you put together, and add them in the sequence on the label.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Post-harvest", "defn": "Post-harvest is all the tasks that you do after you cut the plant. The tasks include drying, trimming, curing and storage.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Moisture content", "defn": "Moisture content is the percentage of the weight of the flower that is water. You use it to measure the yield, and not the safety.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Relative humidity (RH)", "defn": "Relative humidity (RH) is the quantity of water vapor that the air contains, as a percentage from 0 to 100%. You keep the dry room at 60% RH.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Trichomes", "defn": "Trichomes are the glands of resin on the bud. They contain THC, terpenes and flavonoids.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Terpenes", "defn": "Terpenes are oils with an aroma. They give each cultivar its aroma and its flavor. They evaporate if the flower becomes too dry.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Burping", "defn": "Burping is a short opening of a sealed container during curing. The opening lets moist air go out of the container and lets new air go into it.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Curing", "defn": "Curing is the procedure in which a completed batch becomes stable, with the same water activity in all the flower. During curing, you keep the terpenes and prevent mold.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Wet weight", "defn": "Wet weight is the weight of the plants immediately after you cut them and before you dry them. You compare it with the dry weight to get the dry-to-wet ratio.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Dry-to-wet ratio", "defn": "The dry-to-wet ratio is the last dry weight as a fraction of the initial wet weight. It is approximately 10% in the source data.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Bucking", "defn": "Bucking is the procedure in which you cut the buds from the stem. You do it after trimming.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Wet trim / dry trim", "defn": "A wet trim is a trim that you do before drying. A dry trim is a trim that you do after the hang-dry. The source procedure uses a dry trim.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Fan leaves", "defn": "Fan leaves are the large leaves that have a small number of trichomes. You remove them by hand at the start of trimming.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Sugar leaves", "defn": "Sugar leaves are small leaves around the bud with trichomes on them. You cut them from the bud with scissors during trimming.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Stem-snap test", "defn": "The stem-snap test is a test in which you bend a stem to find if the plant is dry. If the stem breaks and does not fold, the plant is almost sufficiently dry.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "ASTM D8197", "defn": "The industry standard for dry cannabis flower gives the range of 0.55 to 0.65 aw.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Two-part nutrient", "defn": "A two-part nutrient is a nutrient system with two parts: Part A and Part B. You must add the parts to the water one after the other. If you mix the parts when they are concentrates, a chemical reaction occurs and you cannot use the batch.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Part A (Pro Core)", "defn": "Part A (Pro Core) is the primary part of Athena Pro Line. It contains a large quantity of calcium and nitrogen. Always dissolve it in the water first.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Part B (Pro Grow / Pro Bloom)", "defn": "Part B (Pro Grow / Pro Bloom) is the second part. It contains sulfates and phosphates. Add it only after Part A dissolves fully and mixes equally in the water.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Mineral salt", "defn": "Mineral salts are the dry crystals of a nutrient powder, as the supplier sends it. They must dissolve in water before a plant can absorb them.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "pH", "defn": "pH is a value on a scale of 0 to 14. It shows how acid or alkaline the water is. You usually adjust a nutrient solution to a pH of approximately 5.8 to 6.2.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Precipitate", "defn": "A precipitate is a solid that comes out of a solution. It occurs when ions that are not compatible, for example calcium with sulfate or phosphate, mix at a high concentration.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Lockout", "defn": "Lockout is the condition in which nutrients are chemically not available to the plant. Here, a precipitate causes it when Part A and Part B mix at a high concentration.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Grams per liter (g/L)", "defn": "Grams for each liter (g/L) is the unit of dose for nutrient powders. To find the grams to weigh, multiply the g/L on the label by the volume of the tank. For example, multiply by 50 for a tank of 50 L.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Dilution", "defn": "Dilution is the procedure in which you mix a material equally in a large volume of water. Thus the concentration is low. This low concentration makes it safe to add Part B after Part A.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Field capacity / substrate EC", "defn": "The substrate EC is the concentration of salt in the substrate of the root zone. The Athena guide gives targets for each stage, for example 3 to 5 in the vegetative stage. You use the mixed feed to increase the substrate EC to these targets.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Internode", "defn": "An internode is the length of bare stem between two nodes.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Cutting", "defn": "A cutting is a shoot that you cut from a plant. It makes roots and becomes a clone. The terms cutting and clone are interchangeable.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Rooting hormone / IBA", "defn": "A rooting hormone is a gel or liquid that contains indole-3-butyric acid (IBA), which is a synthetic auxin. Auxin is a plant hormone for roots. You apply the hormone to a stem after you cut the stem, to cause the stem to make roots.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Rockwool cube", "defn": "A rockwool cube is a plug of mineral fiber that holds water and air. A cutting makes roots in it. A peat plug has the same function.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Dome", "defn": "A dome is a transparent plastic lid for humidity. You put it on a clone tray to keep moisture around the cuttings that do not have roots.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "RH", "defn": "RH is relative humidity. It is the percentage of moisture that the air contains compared with the maximum quantity that the air can contain at that temperature.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "EC", "defn": "EC is electrical conductivity. It shows the strength of the nutrients in the feed water, in mS/cm.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Hardening off", "defn": "Hardening off is the procedure in which you open the vents gradually and remove the dome. Thus the clones with roots adapt to drier room air before transplant.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Air embolism", "defn": "An air embolism is an air bubble in the stem after you cut it. It stops the uptake of water. You can prevent it if you put the cuttings in the substrate quickly and keep the ends of the cuttings wet.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Culling", "defn": "Culling is the procedure in which you divide the clones into groups before transplant. You remove the clones that are weak or do not have roots. The groups are keepers, rejects and kills.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Transplant", "defn": "Transplant is the movement of a clone with roots from its cube into a larger container or block. This starts the vegetative stage.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Topping", "defn": "Topping is the procedure in which you cut the top tip of the plant. The plant then does not become higher, and the branches below the tip become two or more new tops. As a result, the plant becomes wider and has more branches.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "FIM", "defn": "FIM is a topping in which you cut most of the top tip but not all of it. FIM can make some new tops. A topping that removes all the tip makes only two new tops.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Apical dominance", "defn": "Apical dominance is the usual condition in which one primary top of a plant becomes higher than the side branches. Topping and LST stop apical dominance. Thus the growth of the plant is equal in all the branches.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "LST (Low-Stress Training)", "defn": "Low-stress training (LST) is the procedure in which you bend the branches down carefully and attach them in this position. The procedure makes the canopy flat, and you do not cut the plant. The stress is low and there is no recovery time.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Trellis / SCROG", "defn": "A trellis is a horizontal net above the canopy, and the plants become higher through the net. Initially, the net holds the branches apart, and subsequently it holds the heavy buds in position. SCROG is the short name for screen of green.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Spreading", "defn": "Spreading is the procedure in which you pull the branches away from the primary stalk and put them in the openings of the trellis. Thus light and air go to the middle, and the branches fill the canopy equally.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Lollipopping", "defn": "Lollipopping is the procedure in which you remove the small branches, the nodes and the leaves from the bottom third of the plant (approximately the bottom 10 to 18 inches). Thus the energy goes to the top buds, and the plant has the shape of a lollipop.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Defanning / Defoliation", "defn": "Defanning (defoliation) is the removal of selected large fan leaves. It opens the canopy. Thus light goes into the canopy and air flows through it.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Source and sink", "defn": "A source is a leaf with sufficient light that makes more sugar than it uses. A sink is a flower, a root or a leaf in shade that uses more sugar than it makes. When you open the canopy, the sinks that are in shade become sources again.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Larf", "defn": "Larf is a type of flower that is small, has a low density and is not at maturity. It occurs at the bud sites at the bottom of the plant, in shade. It has a low value, and lollipopping removes it.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Shade avoidance response", "defn": "The shade avoidance response is a condition of plants that are too close to each other. The plants use energy to make the weak inner branches longer in the direction of the light. They do not use this energy to make flower of good quality.", "tags": ["reference"]},
]
